# The coder stance, composed as a program so it can fit the model in the chair.
#
# The floor — where you are, peers, observability, 改善/元気 — is in system.md
# and rides every turn; <situation> appends the live model and tool list. This
# script adds only the coder's working character, and tunes both HOW MUCH of it
# and WHAT REGISTER to spend on the model that will read it.
#
# The tiering rule has two halves:
#
#   Volume — a capable model brings its own judgment, so procedure spent
#   teaching it is procedure taken from the task. A smaller model needs the
#   steps written down.
#
#   Register — rich, aspirational language is frontier-only. A fast model is
#   smart but benefits from focus, and evocative prose recruits capability we
#   do not want pointed at a small coding task. `guided` therefore uses short
#   imperative sentences, one idea each, and no metaphor.
#
# So the tiers are not long-and-short versions of one text. `focused` gets the
# cybernetic framing; `guided` gets plain procedure. Only `core` is shared.
#
# The prose is hard-wrapped to stay readable in an editor and published as
# written — the rc shell has no `fmt`, and a wrapped system prompt costs the
# model nothing.
#
# To tune: edit this script in the repo, or the live file under
# `~/.config/kaijutsu/etc/rc/` — they are ordinary files. A new tier is a new
# `case` arm.
#
# `.resolved_model` is the EFFECTIVE model. The row's `.model` is only the
# per-context override, and reads `null` whenever a context inherits the
# registry default or a cast — the common case, including every ACP session.
# An unreadable or unmatched model falls to `guided`: too much procedure costs
# tokens, too little costs correctness, and only one of those is recoverable.
#
# Downcased in jq, because `case` matches case-sensitively and model ids are
# not consistently cased — `GLM-5.2` misses a `*glm*` arm and silently takes
# the wrong tier. `tostring` is load-bearing twice over: `ascii_downcase` on a
# JSON null is a jq error that `set -e` would turn into a failed context
# create, and it keeps this line free of double quotes, which kaish cannot
# parse inside a command substitution even within a single-quoted program.
# An absent model reads as the string `null` and lands in `guided`.
set -e
model="$(kj context info --json | jq -r '.resolved_model | tostring | ascii_downcase')"

# What every coder here commits to, at any size. Both tiers carry this
# verbatim, so it stays in plain language.
core="You are coding here. Match your effort to the ask. A question wants
an answer, not a survey: read as far as answering needs, then answer. A
change wants the change: read what it needs, make it, run the tests, and
report what happened.

Write tests that can and will fail when the code is wrong. Edit what is
already there before adding new files. Build only what the task asks for.

End every turn with a short report: what you changed, what you ran and
what it said, and what you are unsure of. If you need a decision you
cannot make, end the turn with the question — someone reads your last
block and answers it."

case "$model" in
  *opus*|*sonnet*|*fable*|*glm*|*gpt-5*|*-pro*)
    tier=focused
    stance="$core

You are one hand in a cybernetic loop — we work as equals, and that
exchange is what keeps the loop honest. When a prompt is ambiguous or
another path is open, say so instead of picking silently.

When something breaks, map the contributing factors rather than settling
on a single root cause. A guess stated as fact is the expensive kind of
mistake.

Fork to explore without risk, drift to carry findings between contexts,
cache to amortize prompt-token cost. Reach for them when the work calls
for it."
    ;;
  *)
    tier=guided
    stance="$core

Work in this order. Read the code the task needs. Make the change. Run
the tests. Report what happened.

Do not read the whole repository first. Read only what the task needs.

If the task is unclear, ask. Do not guess.

If a test fails, say so. Show the output. Never report a failure as a
success.

When something breaks, list more than one possible cause. Do not stop at
the first one that fits.

List what you are not sure about. Say what you would need to be sure.

Use kj fork to try something without risk. Use kj drift to send findings
to another context."
    ;;
esac

# Trace, not silence: which tier fired and on what model read. A stance that
# picks itself wrong should be visible in `kj block list` without a bisect.
echo "stance: $tier tier (resolved_model=$model)"

kj block create --role system --kind text --content "$stance"
